{"author":"John Wesley","book":"Notes on the Bible","chapter":1048,"classification":{"bucket":"Christian Classics / Commentaries","canonicalStatus":"noncanonical","contentKind":"commentary","cautionReason":"","doctrinalStatus":"historical","labels":["christian-classics","commentaries"],"severity":0},"id":"classics:wesley:Jer.44","lineage":[],"metadata":{"author":"John Wesley","authorshipDateRange":"18th century","genre":"Commentaries","tradition":"Methodist","translator":null,"edition":"CCEL edition; source omits 2 Samuel 24","rights":"Public Domain","source":{"id":"wesley","provider":"CCEL","sourceUrl":"https://www.ccel.org/ccel/wesley/notes.html","title":"Notes on the Bible"}},"originalBook":"Jeremiah","sourceVolumeId":"wesley","source":{"id":"wesley","sourceUrl":"https://www.ccel.org/ccel/wesley/notes.html","title":"Notes on the Bible"},"title":"Jeremiah 44","reference":"Jeremiah 44","notes":[],"verses":[{"verse":1,"text":"Jeremiah represents to the people in Egypt, the former sins and punishment of Judah, ver.","sourceParagraph":1},{"verse":2,"text":"1-10.","sourceParagraph":1},{"verse":3,"text":"He prophesies of their destruction in Egypt, ver.","sourceParagraph":1},{"verse":4,"text":"11-14.","sourceParagraph":1},{"verse":5,"text":"Their obstinacy, ver.","sourceParagraph":1},{"verse":6,"text":"15-19.","sourceParagraph":1},{"verse":7,"text":"Threatened, ver.","sourceParagraph":1},{"verse":8,"text":"20-28.","sourceParagraph":1},{"verse":9,"text":"For a sign, the destruction of Egypt is foretold, ver.","sourceParagraph":1},{"verse":10,"text":"29, 30.","sourceParagraph":1},{"verse":11,"text":"1.","sourceParagraph":2,"scriptureVerseStart":1,"scriptureVerseEnd":1},{"verse":12,"text":"At Migdol - It seems the Jews that went into Egypt had planted themselves at these four places.","sourceParagraph":2,"scriptureVerseStart":1,"scriptureVerseEnd":1},{"verse":13,"text":"Migdol was a city upon the borders of the Red Sea.","sourceParagraph":2,"scriptureVerseStart":1,"scriptureVerseEnd":1},{"verse":14,"text":"Noph was a city, which the Greeks and Latines called Memphis; it is thought to be that, which is now called Cairo.","sourceParagraph":2,"scriptureVerseStart":1,"scriptureVerseEnd":1},{"verse":15,"text":"Pathros was the province, since called Thebais.","sourceParagraph":2,"scriptureVerseStart":1,"scriptureVerseEnd":1},{"verse":16,"text":"14.","sourceParagraph":3,"scriptureVerseStart":14,"scriptureVerseEnd":14},{"verse":17,"text":"For none - Only such shall escape, as have been forced into Egypt against their wills; and as did not fall in with the idolatry of the Egyptians.","sourceParagraph":3,"scriptureVerseStart":14,"scriptureVerseEnd":14},{"verse":18,"text":"15.","sourceParagraph":4,"scriptureVerseStart":15,"scriptureVerseEnd":15},{"verse":19,"text":"All the men - It should seem those that did it were mostly women, and that they did it with some privacy, so that all their husbands did not know of it.","sourceParagraph":4,"scriptureVerseStart":15,"scriptureVerseEnd":15},{"verse":20,"text":"26.","sourceParagraph":5,"scriptureVerseStart":26,"scriptureVerseEnd":26},{"verse":21,"text":"The Lord liveth - There shall not any be left alive of the Jews that are in Egypt, to swear The Lord God liveth.","sourceParagraph":5,"scriptureVerseStart":26,"scriptureVerseEnd":26},{"verse":22,"text":"29.","sourceParagraph":6,"scriptureVerseStart":29,"scriptureVerseEnd":29},{"verse":23,"text":"This shall be a sign - Signs are usually antecedent to the thing signified, but the word is taken in a larger notion in this place for that which should attend the thing signified by it.","sourceParagraph":6,"scriptureVerseStart":29,"scriptureVerseEnd":29}],"scriptureReference":{"book":"Jeremiah","chapter":44}}